Root cause:
The function `rxgk_verify_response()` allocates an `rxgk` context (via `rxgk_get()`) but fails to release it via `rxgk_put()` in two error paths (when `rxgk_decrypt_response()` or `rxgk_verify_authenticator()` fails), causing a memory leak.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
`goto out;` (after `rxgk_decrypt_skb()` fails)
`goto out;` (after `rxgk_verify_authenticator()` fails)
Reason:
These jumps bypass the `rxgk_put(gk)` call, causing a memory leak of the `struct rxgk_context *gk` allocated by `rxgk_generate_transport_key()`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
`goto out_gk` (replacing the two `goto out;` in the error paths)
`out_gk:` followed by `rxgk_put(gk);` (added before the existing `out:` label)
Reason:
By jumping to `out_gk` instead of `out`, the function now calls `rxgk_put(gk)` to release the reference before proceeding to the common cleanup at `out:` (which does `key_put(key)`). This ensures no memory leak in error paths.


